  "notes": "<p><strong>Photo:</strong> Fragment of a painted clay plaque of the 500s BCE, showing a woman in a chariot and Apollo with his lyre. Archaeological Museum of Eleusis, no. 385. Photographed in 2008. <em>Photo Credit: Photo by Zde, CC BY-SA 4.0, via <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Black-figure_plaque,_fragment,_Woman,_Apollo,_6th_century_BC,_AM_eleusis,_081189.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikimedia Commons</a>.</em></p><p><strong>Based on evidence:</strong> The sanctuary of Demeter at Eleusis was on the eastern slope of the town's hill, in the middle of the town rather than on its edge. About 580 to 570 BCE its main terrace was enlarged, and the first hall of initiation was built on it soon after. The painted plaque in the picture was made in the 500s BCE and is kept in the Eleusis museum.</p><h2>Sources</h2><ul><li>Hans Beck, \"Along the Mystery Highway: Eleusis and Thebes in the Archaic Age\", in Hans Beck and Sebastian Scharff, eds., <em>Beyond Mysteries: The Local World of Ancient Eleusis</em> (Brill, 2025), p. 50.</li><li>Painted clay plaque, Archaeological Museum of Eleusis no. 385, as recorded on its Wikimedia Commons page.</li></ul>"
